I wasn't sure about the truth of that assertion so I checked.  Indeed,
you are right.  The decay path isn't direct, but Uranium decays to
lead and the intermediate products are (relatively) ephemeral.

Uranium 238 => Thorium 234 (4.4 billion years)
Thorium 234 => Protactinium 234 (24.1 days)
Protactinium 234 => Uranium 234 (1.17 minutes)

Uranium 234 => Thorium 230 (245,000 years)
Thorium 230 => Radium 226 (75,400 years)
Radium 226 => Radon 222 (1600 years)
Radon 222 => Polonium 218 (3.82 days)
Polonium 218 => Lead 214 (3.11 minutes)

Lead 214 => Bismuth 214 (26.8 minutes)
Bismuth 214 => Polonium 214 (19.9 minutes)
Polonium 214 => Lead 210 (163 microseconds)

Lead 210 => Bismuth 210 (22.3 years)
Bismuth 210 => Polonium 210 (5.01 days)
Polonium 210 => Lead 206 (138 days)

Lead 206 (stable)

